2,4-2=2),(3,0) (not allowed), (4,-2) (not allowed). So L2 covers (1,4) and (2,2). (2,2) already covered by L1. So L2 covers (1,4) and (2,2). So far we have (1,1),(2,2),(1,4). Not great.\n\n- L3: slope -0.5, passing through (1,2) => y - 2 = -0.5 (x - 1) => y = -0.5x + 2.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 2.5 = 1.5 (not integer). At x=3, y = -1.5 + 2.5 = 1 (integer). So L3 passes through (1,2) and (3,1). Good. At x=4, y = -2 + 2.5 = 0.5 (not integer). So L3 covers (1,2),(3,1). Good.\n\n- L4: slope -2, passing through (1,3) => y - 3 = -2 (x - 1) => y = -2x + 5. At x=2, y = -4 + 5 = 1 (integer). So L4 passes through (1,3) and (2,1). At x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1 (not allowed). So L4 covers (1,3),(2,1).\n\nNow we have covered: (1,1),(2,2) by L1; (1,4) by L2; (1,2),(3,1) by L3; (1,3),(2,1) by L4. So far covered points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) all covered; (2,1),(2,2) covered; (3,1) covered. Missing points: (2,3), (3,2), (4,1). Also (2,3) is not covered; (3,2) not covered; (4,1) not covered.\n\nWe have used 4 lines already. So we cannot add more lines. So we need to adjust lines to cover those missing points.\n\nWe could try to modify lines to cover more points. Perhaps we can find lines that cover (2,3) and (3,2) and (4,1) simultaneously.\n\nConsider line L5: slope -1? Not allowed. Slope -2? Let's see line through (2,3) and (4,1): slope (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot use that.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (3,2): slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So cannot use that.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (4,1): slope -1, not allowed. So (2,3) cannot be collinear with any other point in T_4 except maybe (1,4)? Let's check line through (1,4) and (2,3): slope (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So (2,3) cannot be on a sunny line with any other point in T_4 except maybe (3,2)? That's slope -1, not allowed. (4,1) also slope -1. So (2,3) cannot be on a sunny line with any other point in T_4. So (2,3) must be covered by a line that contains only that point (i.e., a line that passes through (2,3) and some other point not in T_4). That's allowed: a line can pass through (2,3) and some other point outside T_4, as long as the line is sunny. So we could have a line that passes through (2,3) and some other point (x0,y0) not in T_4, such that the line is sunny. That line would cover (2,3) and maybe also other points in T_4. But we have only 4 lines total, so we need to allocate them to cover all points. So we could have a line that covers (2,3) and maybe (3,2) or (4,1) if we choose a point outside T_4 that yields a sunny slope.\n\nBut we need to ensure that each line is not horizontal, vertical, or slope -1. So we can choose any other point to define a line.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines that cover all points. Let's try to design them.\n\nWe need to cover the \"hard\" points: (2,3) is isolated because any line through it and any other point in T_4 will have slope -1 (if the other point is (1,4) or (3,2) or (4,1)). So we need to pair (2,3) with a point outside T_4. Similarly, (3,2) is also isolated: any line through (3,2) and any other point in T_4 will have slope -1 (if the other point is (2,3) or (4,1) or (1,1)? Let's check (3,2) with (1,1): slope (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2, which is sunny. So (3,2) can be paired with (1,1) (which is already covered by L1). But we could adjust L1 to also cover (3,2). Let's see: line through (1,1) and (3,2) has slope (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2. Equation: y - 1 = 0.5 (x - 1) => y = 0.5x + 0.5. At x=2, y = 1.5 (not integer). So this line would not pass through any other integer point in T_4 besides (1,1) and (3,2). That's okay. So we could have a line L1: y = 0.5x + 0.5 covering (1,1) and (3,2). That would be sunny (slope 0.5). Good.\n\nNow (4,1) is also isolated: any line through (4,1) and any other point in T_4 will have slope -1 if the other point is (3,2) or (2,3) or (1,4). But we could pair (4,1) with (1,1): slope (1-1)/(4-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. Pair with (2,1): slope (1-1)/(4-2) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. Pair with (3,1): slope (1-1)/(4-3) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. Pair with (1,2): slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, which is sunny (not -1). So line through (1,2) and (4,1) has slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, sunny. So we could have a line covering (1,2) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow (2,3) can be paired with (1,1): slope (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, sunny. So line through (1,1) and (2,3) has slope 2. But (1,1) is already used in L1 maybe. But we can adjust.\n\nLet's try to design 4 lines covering all points:\n\nGoal: cover all points in T_4 with 4 sunny lines.\n\nWe can try to assign each line to cover a pair of points (or maybe a triple) such that each column's points are covered.\n\nWe have columns 1-4. Each line can have at most one point per column. So each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So we need to cover 10 points with 4 lines, average 2.5 points per line. So some lines will cover 3 points, some 2.\n\nWe need to ensure each column's points are covered. Let's list points by column:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2)\nColumn 4: (4,1)\n\nWe need to assign each point to a line.\n\nOne approach: Use vertical lines for some columns? Not allowed because we need all lines sunny. So no vertical lines.\n\nThus each line must be non-vertical.\n\nWe can try to use lines that go through points in column 1 and column 2, maybe also column 3, etc.\n\nLet's try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points.\n\nWe can treat this as a problem of covering a bipartite graph with matchings? Actually each line corresponds to a matching between columns and rows (points). Each line picks at most one point per column, and the points must be collinear.\n\nWe can try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points. Let's try to systematically search.\n\nWe can think of each line as defined by two points (since any line is determined by two points). So we can choose pairs of points that are not vertical/horizontal/slope -1. Then each line will also include any other points that happen to lie on that line (maybe none). So we need to choose up to 4 lines such that the union of points covered includes all points.\n\nWe can try to find a covering manually.\n\nFirst, note that points (2,3), (3,2), (4,1) are collinear with slope -1 (line x+y=5). That's not allowed. So they cannot be on the same line. So each of these points must be covered by a line that does not contain any other point from T_4 (except maybe a point from column 1 that yields a different slope). Let's see:\n\n- (2,3) can be paired with (1,1) (slope 2). That line also passes through (3,5) (outside T_4). So L_a: through (1,1) and (2,3). This line covers (1,1) and (2,3). Good.\n\n- (3,2) can be paired with (1,1) (slope 0.5). But (1,1) already used in L_a. However, a point can be covered by multiple lines; that's allowed. So we could have L_b: through (1,1) and (3,2). That covers (1,1) again (already covered) and (3,2). That's okay.\n\n- (4,1) can be paired with (1,2) (slope -1/3). So L_c: through (1,2) and (4,1). That covers (1,2) and (4,1).\n\nNow we need to cover remaining points: (1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Also (1,1) already covered, (1,2) covered, (2,3) covered, (3,2) covered, (4,1) covered.\n\nWe have used 3 lines so far: L_a, L_b, L_c. We have one line left (since total k=4). We need to cover the remaining points with that line. Let's see if we can find a line that covers all remaining points: (1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's 5 points. A line can have at most one point per column, so it can cover at most one point from column 1, one from column 2, one from column 3, one from column 4. So it cannot cover all 5 points because there are 3 columns among those points: column 1 has (1,3),(1,4) (two points), column 2 has (2,1),(2,2) (two points), column 3 has (3,1) (one point). So we need at least 2 lines to cover column 1's two points and column 2's two points. But we have only one line left. So we need to adjust earlier lines to also cover some of these remaining points.\n\nWe can try to reassign lines to cover more points.\n\nWe have 4 lines total. Let's try to design them to cover all points.\n\nWe need to cover column 1's 4 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Since each line can cover at most one point from column 1, we need at least 4 lines to cover all 4 points in column 1. But we have exactly 4 lines. So each line must cover exactly one point from column 1. So each line must be assigned a distinct point from column 1. So we need to assign each of the 4 lines to one of the points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Then each line can also cover points from other columns.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines, each passing through a distinct point in column 1, and covering all other points.\n\nThus we need to assign:\n\n- L1 passes through (1,1) and maybe some points in columns 2,3,4.\n- L2 passes through (1,2) and maybe some points in columns 2,3,4.\n- L3 passes through (1,3) and maybe some points in columns 2,3,4.\n- L4 passes through (1,4) and maybe some points in columns 2,3,4.\n\nNow we need to cover all points in columns 2,3,4.\n\nColumn 4 has only (4,1). So one of the lines must pass through (4,1). Since each line can have at most one point per column, exactly one line will cover (4,1). So whichever line passes through (4,1) must also pass through a point in column 1 (some (1,i)). So we need a line connecting (1,i) to (4,1) with slope not 0,∞,-1. Let's compute slopes:\n\n- (1,1) to (4,1): slope 0 (horizontal) → not allowed.\n- (1,2) to (4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3 → allowed.\n- (1,3) to (4,1): slope (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3 → allowed.\n- (1,4) to (4,1): slope (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1 → not allowed.\n\nThus the line covering (4,1) must be either L2 (through (1,2)) or L3 (through (1,3)). So we have two options.\n\nNow column 3 has points (3,1) and (3,2). Each must be covered by some line. Since each line can have at most one point per column, we need at least two lines to cover both points in column 3. So two of the lines must pass through (3,1) and (3,2) respectively.\n\nSimilarly, column 2 has points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). We need to cover all three points. Since each line can have at most one point per column, we need at least three lines to cover them. So three of the lines must pass through (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) respectively.\n\nThus we need to assign each of the 4 lines to cover a distinct point in column 1, and also cover a distinct point in column 2 (three lines) and a distinct point in column 3 (two lines) and column 4 (one line). Since we have only 4 lines, we cannot assign each line to cover a point in each column simultaneously. But each line can cover up to 4 points (one per column). So we need to allocate the points across lines such that each line covers at most one point per column.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So total capacity per column is 4 points (since each line can cover at most one point in that column). Column 1 has 4 points, so we need each line to cover exactly one point in column 1. Column 2 has 3 points, so we need 3 of the lines to cover a point in column 2. Column 3 has 2 points, need 2 lines to cover a point in column 3. Column 4 has 1 point, need 1 line to cover it.\n\nThus we need to assign each line to cover a point in column 1, and also possibly a point in column 2, column 3, column 4. Since we have 4 lines, we can assign:\n\n- L1: (1,1) + maybe (2,1) + maybe (3,1) + maybe (4,1) (but (4,1) cannot be with (1,1) because slope 0). So L1 cannot cover (4,1). So L1 can cover (1,1) and maybe (2,1) and maybe (3,1). Let's see if there is a line through (1,1) and (2,1): slope 0, not allowed. So L1 cannot cover (2,1) either. Through (1,1) and (3,1): slope 0, not allowed. So L1 cannot cover any point in column 2 or 3 that has same y=1. So L1 cannot cover any point in column 2 or 3 with y=1. It could cover (2,2) maybe? Let's check line through (1,1) and (2,2): slope 1, allowed. That line also passes through (3,3) (outside T_4). So L1 can cover (1,1) and (2,2). It could also cover (3,? )? At x=3, y = 3? Not in T_4. So L1 can cover (1,1) and (2,2). It cannot cover any point in column 3 because at x=3, y=3 (outside). So L1 can cover (1,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\n- L2: (1,2) maybe with (2,3)? Let's check line through (1,2) and (2,3): slope (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. At x=3, y = 4 (outside). So L2 can cover (1,2) and (2,3). It could also cover (3,? )? At x=3, y = 5 (outside). So L2 cannot cover column 3 or 4.\n\n- L3: (1,3) maybe with (2,1)? Slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. At x=3, y = -3? Actually line equation: y - 3 = -2 (x - 1) => y = -2x + 5. At x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1 (outside). So L3 cannot cover column 3 or 4.\n\n- L4: (1,4) maybe with (2,2)? Slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. At x=3, y = -2*2 + 6 = 2? Wait compute: line through (1,4) and (2,2): slope -2, equation y - 4 = -2 (x - 1) => y = -2x + 6. At x=3, y = -6 + 6 = 0 (outside). So L4 cannot cover column 3 or 4.\n\nThus with these assignments, we cannot cover column 3 points (3,1) and (3,2) at all. So we need to adjust.\n\nWe need to find lines that pass through column 3 points. Let's consider lines that go through (3,1) and some column 1 point. For each column 1 point, compute slope:\n\n- (1,1) to (3,1): slope 0, not allowed.\n- (1,2) to (3,1): slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed.\n- (1,3) to (3,1): slope (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n- (1,4) to (3,1): slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed.\n\nThus lines through (1,2)-(3,1) and (1,4)-(3,1) are allowed. So we could assign L2 to be (1,2)-(3,1). That would cover (1,2) and (3,1). Then L2 cannot also cover (2,3) because that would be a third point in column 2? Actually L2 would have points (1,2) and (3,1). At x=2, the line's y would be? Equation: slope -0.5, line through (1,2): y - 2 = -0.5 (x - 1) => y = -0.5x + 2.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 2.5 = 1.5 (not integer). So L2 does not cover any point in column 2. So L2 covers (1,2) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (3,2). Let's find a line through (3,2) and a column 1 point:\n\n- (1,1) to (3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2, allowed.\n- (1,2) to (3,2): slope 0, not allowed.\n- (1,3) to (3,2): slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed.\n- (1,4) to (3,2): slope (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus lines (1,1)-(3,2) and (1,3)-(3,2) are allowed.\n\nNow we need to cover (4,1). As before, lines (1,2)-(4,1) slope -1/3, allowed; (1,3)-(4,1) slope -2/3, allowed.\n\nNow we need to cover column 2 points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). Each must be covered by a line.\n\nWe have 4 lines total. Let's try to assign them:\n\n- L1: (1,1) with maybe (2,2) (slope 1). That covers (1,1) and (2,2). Could also cover (3,? )? At x=3, y = 3 (outside). So L1 covers (1,1),(2,2).\n\n- L2: (1,2) with (3,1) (slope -0.5). Covers (1,2),(3,1). Good.\n\n- L3: (1,3) with (3,2) (slope -0.5). Wait (1,3)-(3,2) slope -0.5, allowed. That covers (1,3),(3,2). Good.\n\n- L4: (1,4) with (4,1) (slope -1). Not allowed. So we need a different pairing for (1,4). Let's pair (1,4) with (2,1) maybe? Slope (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, allowed. That line passes through (3,? )? At x=3, y = -3*2 + 7? Let's compute: line through (1,4) and (2,1): slope -3, equation y - 4 = -3 (x - 1) => y = -3x + 7. At x=3, y = -9 + 7 = -2 (outside). So L4 covers (1,4) and (2,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered:\n\n- (1,1) covered by L1.\n- (1,2) covered by L2.\n- (1,3) covered by L3.\n- (1,4) covered by L4.\n\n- (2,1) covered by L4.\n- (2,2) covered by L1.\n- (2,3) not yet covered.\n- (3,1) covered by L2.\n- (3,2) covered by L3.\n- (4,1) not yet covered.\n\nWe have used all 4 lines. We still need to cover (2,3) and (4,1). Could we adjust lines to also cover these points? Let's see if any of the lines can be modified to also include (2,3) or (4,1) without breaking other coverage.\n\nCheck L1: currently passes through (1,1) and (2,2). Could we change L1 to pass through (1,1) and (2,3) instead? That would be slope (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, allowed. Then L1 would cover (1,1) and (2,3). At x=3, y = 5 (outside). So L1 would not cover (2,2) then. But (2,2) could be covered by another line maybe L2 or L3 or L4. Let's see.\n\nCheck L2: currently (1,2)-(3,1). Could we adjust L2 to also cover (4,1)? Let's see line through (1,2) and (4,1) slope -1/3, allowed. That line at x=3 gives y = 2 - (1/3)*(3-1) = 2 - 2/3 = 4/3 (not integer). So L2 would not cover (3,1). So we would lose (3,1). But maybe (3,1) could be covered by another line.\n\nCheck L3: currently (1,3)-(3,2). Could we adjust L3 to also cover (4,1)? Let's see line through (1,3) and (4,1) slope (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3, allowed. At x=3, y = 3 + (-2/3)*(3-1) = 3 - 4/3 = 5/3 (not integer). So L3 would not cover (3,2). So we would lose (3,2).\n\nCheck L4: currently (1,4)-(2,1). Could we adjust L4 to also cover (4,1)? Let's see line through (1,4) and (4,1) slope -1, not allowed. So no.\n\nThus we need to find a configuration where each line covers a distinct column 1 point, and also covers the other points. Let's try a systematic approach: we need to cover (2,3) and (4,1). Since (4,1) can only be paired with (1,2) or (1,3) (as we saw). So one of the lines must be either (1,2)-(4,1) or (1,3)-(4,1). Let's pick (1,2)-(4,1) as L2. Then L2 covers (1,2) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (2,3). (2,3) can be paired with (1,1) (slope 2) or (1,4) (slope -1/3? Actually (1,4)-(2,3) slope (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed). So only (1,1) works. So we need a line L1 that passes through (1,1) and (2,3). That line slope 2, allowed. So L1 covers (1,1) and (2,3). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (3,1) and (3,2). (3,1) can be paired with (1,2) (slope -0.5) but (1,2) already used in L2 (which is (1,2)-(4,1)). However, a point can be covered by multiple lines, but we need to ensure each line covers at most one point per column. L2 already covers column 1 point (1,2) and column 4 point (4,1). It cannot also cover column 3 point (3,1) because that would be a third point in a different column, which is allowed (since each line can have at most one point per column, but can have points in multiple columns). Actually L2 currently has points (1,2) (col 1) and (4,1) (col 4). It could also have a point in column 3 (col 3) as long as the line passes through that point. Let's check if line through (1,2) and (4,1) also passes through (3,1). The line equation: slope -1/3, y - 2 = -1/3 (x - 1) => y = -1/3 x + 7/3. At x=3, y = -1 + 7/3 = 4/3, not 1. So (3,1) not on that line. So L2 cannot cover (3,1). So we need another line to cover (3,1). That line must pass through a column 1 point not already used? Actually we have column 1 points (1,1) used by L1, (1,2) used by L2. We still have (1,3) and (1,4) available for L3 and L4. So we can assign L3 to cover (1,3) and (3,1). Let's check slope: (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So (1,3)-(3,1) is slope -1, not allowed. So we cannot pair (1,3) with (3,1). How about (1,4)-(3,1)? Slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed. So L3 could be (1,4)-(3,1). That covers (1,4) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (3,2). We have left column 1 point (1,3) for L4. Let's see if we can pair (1,3) with (3,2). Slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed. So L4: (1,3)-(3,2). That covers (1,3) and (3,2). Good.\n\nNow we have covered all points? Let's list:\n\n- L1: (1,1),(2,3)\n- L2: (1,2),(4,1)\n- L3: (1,4),(3,1)\n- L4: (1,3),(3,2)\n\nNow check column 2 points: (2,1),(2,2) are not covered. Indeed we have not covered (2,1) and (2,2). So we need to cover those as well. But we have used all 4 lines. So we need to adjust to also cover (2,1) and (2,2). Perhaps we can modify some lines to also include a point in column 2.\n\nLet's see if any of the lines can also pass through a column 2 point without breaking other coverage.\n\n- L1: passes through (1,1) and (2,3). At x=2, y=3 (already). At x=2, we have (2,3). At x=2, we cannot also have (2,1) or (2,2). So L1 cannot cover any other column 2 point.\n\n- L2: passes through (1,2) and (4,1). At x=2, y = -1/3*2 + 7/3 = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3, not integer. So L2 does not cover any column 2 point.\n\n- L3: passes through (1,4) and (3,1). Equation: slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5. Equation: y - 4 = -1.5 (x - 1) => y = -1.5x + 5.5. At x=2, y = -3 + 5.5 = 2.5, not integer. So L3 does not cover column 2 point.\n\n- L4: passes through (1,3) and (3,2). Slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -0.5. Equation: y - 3 = -0.5 (x - 1) => y = -0.5x + 3.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5, not integer. So L4 does not cover column 2 point.\n\nThus none of the lines cover (2,1) or (2,2). So we need to adjust.\n\nWe could try to pair (2,1) or (2,2) with a column 1 point, but we already used all column 1 points. However, a point can be covered by multiple lines, so we could have a line that covers a column 1 point already used, but also covers a column 2 point. But each line can have at most one point per column, but can have multiple points across columns. So we could modify L1 to also cover (2,2) maybe? But L1 already has (2,3) as its column 2 point. So cannot have two column 2 points.\n\nThus we need to reassign column 2 points across lines differently.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each can cover at most one column 2 point. There are 3 column 2 points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). So we need to assign each of these to a distinct line. In our current assignment, L1 covers (2,3). L2, L3, L4 cover none. So we need to adjust L2, L3, L4 to each cover one of (2,1) and (2,2). Since we have 2 remaining column 2 points, we need to assign them to two of the lines. Let's try to adjust L2 to cover (2,1) while still covering (1,2) and (4,1). Is there a line that passes through (1,2), (2,1), and (4,1)? Let's check if (1,2),(2,1) define a line: slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So cannot have (1,2)-(2,1) line. So L2 cannot cover (2,1) if it also covers (1,2). Could L2 cover (2,2) instead? (1,2)-(2,2) slope 0, not allowed. So L2 cannot cover any column 2 point while covering (1,2). So we need to assign (2,1) and (2,2) to other lines.\n\nConsider L3: currently (1,4)-(3,1). Could we adjust L3 to also pass through (2,2)? Let's see if (1,4),(2,2),(3,1) are collinear? Compute slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not equal. So not collinear. So L3 cannot pass through (2,2) while also passing through (1,4) and (3,1). Could we change L3 to pass through (1,4) and (2,2) (slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed). Then line equation: y - 4 = -2 (x - 1) => y = -2x + 6. At x=3, y = -6 + 6 = 0, not (3,1). So L3 would not cover (3,1). So we would need to cover (3,1) elsewhere.\n\nConsider L4: currently (1,3)-(3,2). Could we adjust L4 to also pass through (2,1) maybe? Let's see if (1,3),(2,1),(3,2) are collinear? Compute slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, slope (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, not equal. So not collinear. Could we adjust L4 to pass through (1,3) and (2,1)? Slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. Equation: y - 3 = -2 (x - 1) => y = -2x + 5. At x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1, not (3,2). So L4 would not cover (3,2). So we need to cover (3,2) elsewhere.\n\nThus we need to reassign lines more flexibly.\n\nLet's try a different approach: maybe we can find a covering with 4 lines that works. Let's try to use a systematic search approach: we need to assign each column 1 point to a line, and each line can also cover at most one point from each other column. So we need to assign points from columns 2,3,4 to lines such that each line gets at most one point per column, and each point is assigned to some line.\n\nWe can treat this as a bipartite matching problem: we have lines L1..L4, each line has a column 1 point (distinct). For each column c>1, we need to assign each point in that column to a line such that the line's column 1 point and the point are collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nThus we need to find a matching between points in column c and lines such that each line gets at most one point from column c, and the line's column 1 point is collinear with that point.\n\nThus we need to find for each column c a matching between its points and the set of column 1 points such that the line connecting them is sunny (i.e., slope not 0,∞,-1). Since we have 4 column 1 points, we can assign each column's points to distinct column 1 points (or reuse column 1 points across columns, but each line can have at most one point per column, but can have points from multiple columns). So we need to assign each point in column c to a column 1 point (i.e., a line) such that the slope is allowed.\n\nThus we need to find a set of assignments for columns 2,3,4.\n\nLet's denote column 1 points: A1=(1,1), A2=(1,2), A3=(1,3), A4=(1,4). For each point P in column c (c=2,3,4), we need to assign it to some Ai such that the line Ai-P is sunny.\n\nWe can compute slopes:\n\nColumn 2 points: B1=(2,1), B2=(2,2), B3=(2,3).\n\n- Slope from A1 (1,1) to B1 (2,1): 0 (horizontal) → not allowed.\n- A1 to B2: (2-1)/(2-1) = 1 → allowed.\n- A1 to B3: (3-1)/(2-1) = 2 → allowed.\n\n- A2 (1,2) to B1: (1-2)/(2-1) = -1 → not allowed.\n- A2 to B2: (2-2)/(2-1) = 0 → not allowed.\n- A2 to B3: (3-2)/(2-1) = 1 → allowed.\n\n- A3 (1,3) to B1: (1-3)/(2-1) = -2 → allowed.\n- A3 to B2: (2-3)/(2-1) = -1 → not allowed.\n- A3 to B3: (3-3)/(2-1) = 0 → not allowed.\n\n- A4 (1,4) to B1: (1-4)/(2-1) = -3 → allowed.\n- A4 to B2: (2-4)/(2-1) = -2 → allowed.\n- A4 to B3: (3-4)/(2-1) = -1 → not allowed.\n\nThus allowed assignments for column 2:\n\n- B1 can be assigned to A3 or A4.\n- B2 can be assigned to A1 or A4.\n- B3 can be assigned to A1 or A2.\n\nNow column 3 points: C1=(3,1), C2=(3,2).\n\nCompute slopes:\n\n- A1 (1,1) to C1 (3,1): slope (1-1)/(3-1) = 0 → not allowed.\n- A1 to C2: (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2 = 0.5 → allowed.\n\n- A2 (1,2) to C1: (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5 → allowed.\n- A2 to C2: (2-2)/(3-1) = 0 → not allowed.\n\n- A3 (1,3) to C1: (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1 → not allowed.\n- A3 to C2: (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5 → allowed.\n\n- A4 (1,4) to C1: (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5 → allowed.\n- A4 to C2: (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1 → not allowed.\n\nThus allowed assignments for column 3:\n\n- C1 can be assigned to A2 or A4.\n- C2 can be assigned to A1 or A3.\n\nNow column 4 point: D1=(4,1).\n\nCompute slopes:\n\n- A1 (1,1) to D1 (4,1): slope (1-1)/(4-1) = 0 → not allowed.\n- A2 (1,2) to D1: (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333 → allowed.\n- A3 (1,3) to D1: (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3 ≈ -0.667 → allowed.\n- A4 (1,4) to D1: (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1 → not allowed.\n\nThus D1 can be assigned to A2 or A3.\n\nNow we need to assign each point to a line (i.e., to a column 1 point) such that each line (Ai) gets at most one point from each column. Since we have 4 lines, each Ai can be assigned at most one point from each column. So we need to find a matching for each column.\n\nWe can treat each column separately: we need to assign each point in column 2 to a distinct Ai (or possibly same Ai as long as each Ai gets at most one point from column 2). Since there are 3 points in column 2 and 4 lines, we can assign them to distinct Ai's. Similarly for column 3 (2 points) and column 4 (1 point). The assignments across columns are independent (Ai can be assigned a point from each column). So we just need to find a feasible assignment for each column.\n\nLet's try to find assignments:\n\nColumn 2: B1 -> A3 or A4; B2 -> A1 or A4; B3 -> A1 or A2.\n\nWe need to assign B1, B2, B3 to distinct Ai's. Let's try:\n\n- Assign B1 to A3.\n- Assign B2 to A1.\n- Assign B3 to A2.\n\nCheck allowed: B1->A3 allowed, B2->A1 allowed, B3->A2 allowed. All distinct Ai's: A1, A2, A3 used. A4 unused. Good.\n\nColumn 3: C1 -> A2 or A4; C2 -> A1 or A3.\n\nWe need to assign C1 and C2 to distinct Ai's (they can reuse Ai's used in column 2, that's fine). Let's try:\n\n- Assign C1 to A4 (allowed).\n- Assign C2 to A1 (allowed).\n\nNow column 4: D1 -> A2 or A3.\n\nWe need to assign D1 to some Ai. Let's assign D1 to A2 (allowed). So far assignments:\n\n- A1: B2 (col2), C2 (col3)\n- A2: B3 (col2), D1 (col4)\n- A3: B1 (col2)\n- A4: C1 (col3)\n\nCheck each Ai's assignments per column: A1 has B2 (col2) and C2 (col3). That's okay (different columns). A2 has B3 (col2) and D1 (col4). OK. A3 has B1 (col2). OK. A4 has C1 (col3). OK.\n\nNow we need to ensure that each line defined by Ai and its assigned points is consistent: For each Ai, the line must pass through all assigned points (Ai plus each assigned point). Since a line is determined by two points, if we assign more than one point to the same line (i.e., Ai assigned to points from different columns), we need to check that those points are collinear with Ai. For each Ai, we have at most one point per column, but we could have up to 3 points (one from each of columns 2,3,4). However, a line can only pass through at most two points (unless they are collinear). So if we assign more than one point to the same Ai, we need to ensure that all those points lie on the same line with Ai. That is a stronger condition: each line can have at most one point per column, but also all points assigned to that line must be collinear. So we cannot arbitrarily assign points to Ai; they must be collinear.\n\nThus we need to find for each Ai a set of points (maybe from multiple columns) that are collinear with Ai. Since each line can have at most one point per column, but can have multiple points across columns if they are collinear.\n\nThus we need to find a line through Ai that passes through some points in columns 2,3,4. For each Ai, we can choose at most one point from each column, but they must be collinear.\n\nThus we need to find a line through Ai that passes through a point in column 2, maybe also a point in column 3, maybe also a point in column 4, all collinear.\n\nThus we need to find for each Ai a line that passes through Ai and possibly other points.\n\nThus the assignment problem is more complex: we need to find a set of lines L1..L4, each passing through a distinct Ai, such that each point in T_4 is on some line.\n\nWe can try to find such lines manually.\n\nGiven the allowed slopes we computed earlier, we can try to find lines that pass through Ai and a point in column 2, and also maybe a point in column 3, etc.\n\nLet's list all possible lines from each Ai to points in column 2,3,4:\n\nFrom A1 (1,1):\n- To B2 (2,2): slope 1, line L1: y = x.\n- To B3 (2,3): slope 2, line L1': y = 2x - 1? Actually line through (1,1) and (2,3): slope 2, equation y - 1 = 2(x - 1) => y = 2x - 1.\n- To C2 (3,2): slope 0.5, line L1'': y = 0.5x + 0.5.\n- To D1 (4,1): slope 0, not allowed.\n\nFrom A2 (1,2):\n- To B3 (2,3): slope 1, line y = x + 1? Actually line through (1,2) and (2,3): slope 1, equation y = x + 1.\n- To C1 (3,1): slope -0.5, line y = -0.5x + 2.5.\n- To D1 (4,1): slope -1/3, line y = -1/3 x + 7/3.\n\nFrom A3 (1,3):\n- To B1 (2,1): slope -2, line y = -2x + 5.\n- To C2 (3,2): slope -0.5, line y = -0.5x + 3.5.\n- To D1 (4,1): slope -2/3, line y = -2/3 x + 11/3.\n\nFrom A4 (1,4):\n- To B1 (2,1): slope -3, line y = -3x + 7.\n- To B2 (2,2): slope -2, line y = -2x + 6.\n- To C1 (3,1): slope -1.5, line y = -1.5x + 5.5.\n\nNow we need to pick up to 4 lines (one per Ai) that cover all points. Each line can be any of the above possibilities (or possibly also pass through a point in column 3 or 4). Let's see if we can cover all points with 4 lines.\n\nWe have points to cover: B1, B2, B3, C1, C2, D1.\n\nWe need to assign each point to a line.\n\nLet's consider using lines:\n\n- L1: A1-B2 (y = x). This covers A1 (1,1) and B2 (2,2). It also passes through (3,3) (outside) and (4,4) (outside). So L1 covers B2.\n\n- L2: A2-D1 (y = -1/3 x + 7/3). This covers A2 (1,2) and D1 (4,1). It also passes through (2,5/3) (not integer), (3,4/3) (not integer). So L2 covers D1.\n\n- L3: A3-B1 (y = -2x + 5). This covers A3 (1,3) and B1 (2,1). At x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1 (outside). So L3 covers B1.\n\n- L4: A4-C1 (y = -1.5x + 5.5). This covers A4 (1,4) and C1 (3,1). At x=2, y = -3 + 5.5 = 2.5 (not integer). So L4 covers C1.\n\nNow we have covered B2, D1, B1, C1. Remaining points: B3 (2,3), C2 (3,2). Also we need to cover A1, A2, A3, A4 (but they are covered as part of lines). So we need to cover B3 and C2. We have used all 4 lines. Could we adjust some lines to also cover B3 or C2? Let's see.\n\n- L1 (A1-B2) currently covers B2. Could we change L1 to cover B3 instead? L1': A1-B3 (y = 2x - 1). This line passes through (2,3) and (1,1). At x=3, y = 5 (outside). So L1' would cover B3 instead of B2. Then B2 would be uncovered. Could we cover B2 elsewhere? Maybe L2 or L3 or L4 could be changed to cover B2.\n\n- L2 (A2-D1) currently covers D1. Could we change L2 to cover B2? A2-B2 slope 0, not allowed. So cannot.\n\n- L3 (A3-B1) could be changed to cover B2? A3-B2 slope -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\n- L4 (A4-C1) could be changed to cover B2? A4-B2 slope -2, allowed. That line would be A4-B2: slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, equation y = -2x + 6. At x=3, y = 0 (outside). So L4 could be changed to cover B2 instead of C1. Then C1 would be uncovered.\n\nThus we could try to reassign lines to cover B3 and C2.\n\nLet's see if any line can cover C2 (3,2). Options: A1-C2 (slope 0.5), A3-C2 (slope -0.5). So we could have a line L5: A1-C2 (y = 0.5x + 0.5). That would cover A1 and C2. But we already have L1 using A1. Could we replace L1 with this line? Then L1 would cover C2 instead of B2. Then B2 would be uncovered. Could we cover B2 elsewhere? Maybe L2 or L3 or L4 could be changed to cover B2. Let's see:\n\n- L2 (A2-D1) cannot cover B2 (horizontal). L3 (A3-B1) cannot cover B2 (slope -1). L4 (A4-C1) cannot cover B2 (slope -2). Actually L4 could be changed to A4-B2 (slope -2). That would cover B2. So we could have:\n\nL1: A1-C2 (covers C2)\nL2: A2-D1 (covers D1)\nL3: A3-B1 (covers B1)\nL4: A4-B2 (covers B2)\n\nNow we have covered B1, B2, C2, D1. Remaining points: B3 (2,3), C1 (3,1). Also we need to cover A1, A2, A3, A4 (they are covered as part of lines). So we need to cover B3 and C1. But we have used all 4 lines. Could we adjust some line to also cover B3 or C1? Let's see:\n\n- L1 (A1-C2) currently passes through (1,1) and (3,2). At x=2, y = 0.5*2 + 0.5 = 1.5 (not integer). So L1 does not cover B3.\n\n- L2 (A2-D1) passes through (1,2) and (4,1). At x=2, y = -1/3*2 + 7/3 = 5/3 (not integer). So L2 does not cover B3 or C1.\n\n- L3 (A3-B1) passes through (1,3) and (2,1). At x=3, y = -2*3 + 5 = -1 (outside). So L3 does not cover C1.\n\n- L4 (A4-B2) passes through (1,4) and (2,2). At x=3, y = -2*3 + 6 = 0 (outside). So L4 does not cover C1.\n\nThus B3 and C1 remain uncovered.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe need to find a different set of lines that can cover all points. Let's try to find a covering with 4 lines using a systematic approach.\n\nWe can treat each line as a pair (Ai, P) where Ai is a column 1 point, P is a point in some column >1. The line may also pass through a point in another column if collinear.\n\nThus we need to find up to 4 lines such that each point in T_4 is on at least one line.\n\nWe can try to search manually for a covering.\n\nGiven the small size, we could try to enumerate possibilities. But we need to see if any covering exists. If we can prove that no covering exists, then k=4 is impossible, supporting the solution's claim. If we find a covering, then the solution's answer is wrong.\n\nGiven the difficulty, we need to be thorough. Let's attempt to find a covering systematically.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each must contain a distinct column 1 point (since each column 1 point must be covered, and each line can cover at most one column 1 point). Actually it's possible that a line could cover two column 1 points? No, because column 1 points have same x-coordinate (x=1). Two distinct points with same x-coordinate would be vertical line, which is not sunny. So a sunny line cannot contain two points from column 1. So each column 1 point must be on a distinct line. So we have exactly 4 lines, each containing exactly one of the column 1 points.\n\nThus each line is determined by its column 1 point and at least one other point (maybe none, but then line would be just that point, but a line must be defined by at least two points; but we could define a line through a column 1 point and some other point not in T_4, but that line would still be a line. So each line must be defined by at least two points, but we can choose any second point (maybe not in T_4). However, we need to cover all points in T_4. So each line must be chosen to cover as many points as possible.\n\nThus each line is of the form L_i: line through Ai and some other point Q_i (maybe not in T_4). The line will intersect each column at at most one point. So for each column c>1, the line may intersect column c at some integer point (if the line passes through an integer point in that column). So we need to choose Q_i such that the line passes through integer points in as many columns as possible.\n\nThus we need to find for each Ai a line that passes through integer points in columns 2,3,4 (maybe all three). Ideally each line would cover one point in each of columns 2,3,4, covering all points. Since we have 4 lines, we could cover up to 4*3 = 12 points across columns 2-4, but we only have 6 points there, so we have enough capacity. However, we need to ensure each point is covered exactly once (or at least once). So we need to find lines L_i such that the set of points covered across columns 2-4 is exactly the set of points B1,B2,B3,C1,C2,D1.\n\nThus we need to find a set of 4 lines, each passing through Ai and some integer points in columns 2-4, such that each of the 6 points appears on at least one line.\n\nWe can try to find lines that cover multiple points across columns. For example, a line through A1 (1,1) and B2 (2,2) also passes through C3? Actually column 3 point (3,3) is not in T_4. So that line only covers B2.\n\nLine through A1 and B3 (2,3) passes through C4? At x=3, y = 5 (outside). So only covers B3.\n\nLine through A1 and C2 (3,2) passes through B? At x=2, y = 1.5 (not integer). So only covers C2.\n\nLine through A1 and D1 (4,1) is horizontal, not allowed.\n\nThus any line through A1 can cover at most one point among B2, B3, C2. So A1 can cover at most one of those points.\n\nSimilarly, A2 can cover at most one of B3, C1, D1 (since slopes to those points are allowed). Let's check:\n\n- A2 to B3: slope 1, line passes through (1,2),(2,3). At x=3, y = 4 (outside). So covers B3 only.\n\n- A2 to C1: slope -0.5, line passes through (1,2),(3,1). At x=2, y = 1.5 (not integer). So covers C1 only.\n\n- A2 to D1: slope -1/3, line passes through (1,2),(4,1). At x=2, y = 5/3 (not integer). So covers D1 only.\n\nThus A2 can cover at most one of B3, C1, D1.\n\nA3 can cover at most one of B1, C2, D1.\n\n- A3 to B1: slope -2, line passes through (1,3),(2,1). At x=3, y = -1 (outside). So covers B1 only.\n\n- A3 to C2: slope -0.5, line passes through (1,3),(3,2). At x=2, y = 2 (integer!). Wait compute: line through (1,3) and (3,2): slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5. Equation: y - 3 = -0.5 (x - 1) => y = -0.5x + 3.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5 (not integer). Actually 2.5, not integer. So does not cover B2. So only covers C2.\n\n- A3 to D1: slope -2/3, line passes through (1,3),(4,1). At x=2, y = -2/3*2 + 11/3 = -4/3 + 11/3 = 7/3 (not integer). So only covers D1.\n\nThus A3 can cover at most one of B1, C2, D1.\n\nA4 can cover at most one of B1, B2, C1.\n\n- A4 to B1: slope -3, line passes through (1,4),(2,1). At x=3, y = -6 + 7 = 1 (integer!). Wait compute: line through (1,4) and (2,1): slope (1-4)/(2-1) = -3. Equation: y - 4 = -3 (x - 1) => y = -3x + 7. At x=3, y = -9 + 7 = -2 (outside). Actually -2, not integer in T_4. So only covers B1.\n\n- A4 to B2: slope -2, line passes through (1,4),(2,2). At x=3, y = -2*3 + 6 = 0 (outside). So only covers B2.\n\n- A4 to C1: slope -1.5, line passes through (1,4),(3,1). At x=2, y = -1.5*2 + 5.5 = 2.5 (not integer). So only covers C1.\n\nThus each line can cover at most one point from columns 2-4. So each line can cover at most one point besides its column 1 point. So with 4 lines, we can cover at most 4 points from columns 2-4. But we have 6 points to cover. Therefore it's impossible to cover all points with 4 sunny lines. This is a crucial observation: each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, but also each line can intersect each column at most once, but we need to cover all points. However, we have 4 lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So for column 2 (3 points), we need at least 3 lines covering them. For column 3 (2 points), we need at least 2 lines covering them. For column 4 (1 point), we need at least 1 line covering it. So total lines needed to cover all points across columns 2-4 is at least max(3,2,1) = 3? Actually we need to cover each point, but a line can cover at most one point per column, but a line can cover points from multiple columns. So we could have a line that covers a point in column 2 and a point in column 3, etc. So the lower bound on number of lines is at least the size of a maximum matching in a bipartite graph? Actually we need to cover all points with lines, each line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover points from multiple columns. So we need to find a set of lines such that each point is assigned to a line, and each line can have at most one point per column. This is like a hypergraph covering problem. The minimal number of lines needed is at least the size of a largest column (since each line can cover at most one point per column). But we have 4 lines, and the largest column (column 2) has 3 points, so 4 lines is enough. However, we also need to ensure that each line can be defined by a column 1 point and the assigned points are collinear. The earlier analysis shows that each line can cover at most one point from columns 2-4, because for each Ai, the slopes to points in columns 2-4 are distinct and not collinear with each other. Let's verify: For each Ai, the slopes to points in columns 2-4 are distinct, but could there be a line through Ai that passes through two points from different columns? For example, line through A1 (1,1) and B2 (2,2) also passes through C3? No, C3 is (3,3) not in T_4. So not relevant. But maybe line through A1 and B2 also passes through D1? No, D1 is (4,1). So no. So each line can cover at most one point from columns 2-4. However, is there any line through Ai that passes through two points from columns 2-4? Let's check for each Ai:\n\n- For A1 (1,1): possible points: B2 (2,2), B3 (2,3), C2 (3,2). Are any two of these collinear with A1? Let's check slopes:\n\n  - A1-B2 slope 1.\n  - A1-B3 slope 2.\n  - A1-C2 slope 0.5.\n\nAll distinct, so no line through A1 can pass through two of them.\n\n- For A2 (1,2): points: B3 (2,3) slope 1; C1 (3,1) slope -0.5; D1 (4,1) slope -1/3. All distinct.\n\n- For A3 (1,3): points: B1 (2,1) slope -2; C2 (3,2) slope -0.5; D1 (4,1) slope -2/3. Distinct.\n\n- For A4 (1,4): points: B1 (2,1) slope -3; B2 (2,2) slope -2; C1 (3,1) slope -1.5. Distinct.\n\nThus indeed each line can cover at most one point from columns 2-4. So with 4 lines, we can cover at most 4 points from columns 2-4. But we have 6 points to cover. Therefore it's impossible to cover all points with 4 sunny lines. This proves that k=4 is impossible for n=4. Since for any n≥4, we can reduce to the subproblem of covering the rightmost 4 columns (i.e., columns n-3,...,n) which is isomorphic to T_4 (by translation). So k≥4 is impossible for any n≥4. For n=3, k can be at most 3 (since n lines total). But we need to check if k=3 is possible for n=3. The solution gave a construction for k=3 for any n≥3: they used three sunny lines covering the six points (n=3 case). Let's check if that works for n=3. For n=3, T_3 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's 6 points. The solution's construction for k=3 uses lines L1, L2, L3 defined as:\n\nL1: y-1 = -1/2 (x - n) => for n=3, L1: y-1 = -1/2 (x - 3) => y = -0.5x + 2.5. This line passes through (3,1) and (1,2)? Let's check: x=3 => y = -1.5 + 2.5 = 1, good. x=1 => y = -0.5 + 2.5 = 2, so (1,2) is on L1. So L1 covers (3,1) and (1,2). Good.\n\nL2: y-1 = -2 (x - (n-1)) => n-1 = 2 => y-1 = -2 (x - 2) => y = -2x + 5. This line passes through (2,1) and (1,3)? Let's check: x=2 => y = -4 + 5 = 1, good. x=1 => y = -2 + 5 = 3, so (1,3) is on L2. So L2 covers (2,1) and (1,3).\n\nL3: y-1 = (x - (n-2)) => n-2 = 1 => y-1 = (x - 1) => y = x. This line passes through (1,1) and (2,2) and (3,3) (outside). So L3 covers (1,1) and (2,2). So the three sunny lines cover (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's all points. So k=3 works for n=3.\n\nThus the answer {0,1,3} is correct for n=3.\n\nNow we need to verify that k=0 and k=1 constructions are valid for all n≥3. k=0: vertical lines x=1,...,x=n. That's fine. k=1: vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1 plus sunny line L: y-1 = (1/(1-n))(x-n). Let's check that line passes through (n,1). Indeed plugging x=n gives y-1 = (1/(1-n))*0 = 0 => y=1. So (n,1) is covered. For any a ≤ n-1, point (a,b) lies on vertical line x=a. So all points covered. The slope of L is 1/(1-n). Since n≥3, denominator is negative, slope is negative but not -1 (since 1/(1-n) = -1/(n-1). For n=3, slope = -1/2, not -1. So L is sunny. So k=1 works.\n\nk=3: The construction uses three sunny lines L1, L2, L3 as defined, plus vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3. For n≥3, n-3 may be zero (for n=3). For n=3, there are no vertical lines, just the three sunny lines. For n=4, we have vertical line x=1 (since n-3 = 1). The three sunny lines cover the six points in columns 2,3,4 (the rightmost three columns). Let's verify for n=4: The points are (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); (3,1),(3,2); (4,1). The three sunny lines as defined for n=4:\n\n- L1: y-1 = -1/2 (x - 4) => y = -0.5x + 3. So L1 passes through (4,1) and (2,2)? Let's check: x=4 => y= -2 + 3 = 1, good. x=2 => y = -1 + 3 = 2, so (2,2) is on L1. Also x=3 => y = -1.5 + 3 = 1.5 (not integer). So L1 covers (4,1) and (2,2). Wait earlier they said L1 goes through (n,1) and (n-2,2). For n=4, n-2=2, so (2,2) indeed. So L1 covers (4,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\n- L2: y-1 = -2 (x - (n-1)) => n-1=3 => y-1 = -2 (x - 3) => y = -2x + 7. This passes through (3,1) and (2,3)? Let's check: x=3 => y = -6 + 7 = 1, good. x=2 => y = -4 + 7 = 3, so (2,3) is on L2. So L2 covers (3,1) and (2,3).\n\n- L3: y-1 = (x - (n-2)) => n-2=2 => y-1 = (x - 2) => y = x - 1. This passes through (2,1) and (3,2)? Let's check: x=2 => y = 1, good. x=3 => y = 2, good. So L3 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Also passes through (4,3) (outside). So L3 covers (2,1) and (3,2).\n\nThus the three sunny lines cover points: (4,1),(2,2),(3,1),(2,3),(2,1),(3,2). That's six points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). The remaining points are column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). These are covered by vertical line x=1. So the construction works for n=4.\n\nThus the constructions are valid.\n\nNow we need to verify the upper bound arguments for k≥4 and k=2. The solution's argument for k=2: they argue that if k≤2, column n-2 (size 3) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines, so we need a vertical line at x=n-2. Then they argue that if we do not use a vertical line at x=n-1, both its points must be covered by sunny lines, each sunny line must contain exactly one of them. Then they argue that point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point of column n-1, because any line joining (n,1) with either of the two points of column n-1 is either horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny. Hence (n,1) would require a third sunny line, contradicting k≤2. This argument seems correct.\n\nBut we need to check if there is any alternative: Could we use a vertical line at x=n-1 instead of x=n-2? The argument says column n-2 has size 3, so we need a vertical line at x=n-2. But could we use a vertical line at x=n-2 and also a vertical line at x=n-1? That would be two vertical lines, which are non-sunny. Then we would have k=2 sunny lines left (since total lines n). But the argument says we need at least one vertical line for column n-2, but we could also have a vertical line for column n-1, which would reduce the need for sunny lines covering column n-1. However, the argument says \"If we do not use a vertical line x=n-1, both its points must be covered by sunny lines\". But we could use a vertical line x=n-1, which is allowed (non-sunny). Then we would have vertical lines at x=n-2 and x=n-1, using two non-sunny lines. Then we would have n-2 remaining lines, of which k=2 are sunny, so n-2-2 = n-4 non-sunny lines left (vertical/horizontal/slope -1). But we need to cover column n (size 1) and columns 1..n-3. The vertical lines at x=n-2 and x=n-1 cover those columns. So we have n-4 remaining lines to cover columns 1..n-3. Since we have k=2 sunny lines, we have 2 sunny lines and n-4 non-sunny lines left. The non-sunny lines could be vertical lines covering columns 1..n-3, but we have only n-4 non-sunny lines, which is exactly the number of columns 1..n-3 (since there are n-3 columns). Actually columns 1..n-3 are n-3 columns. We have n-4 non-sunny lines left, which is one fewer than needed to cover each column with a vertical line. However, we could use sunny lines to cover some points in those columns. Since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, we could use the 2 sunny lines to cover one point in each of the n-3 columns, reducing the need for vertical lines by at most 2 columns. So we would need vertical lines for the remaining n-5 columns. But we have n-4 non-sunny lines, which is enough to cover n-5 columns (since n-4 >= n-5). So maybe k=2 is possible? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe solution's argument for k=2 seems to assume that we must have a vertical line at x=n-2, and then they consider column n-1. They argue that if we do not use a vertical line at x=n-1, then both its points must be covered by sunny lines, each sunny line must contain exactly one of them. Then they argue that (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point of column n-1, so (n,1) would require a third sunny line, contradicting k≤2. However, they didn't consider the possibility of using a vertical line at x=n-1. If we use a vertical line at x=n-1, then we would have vertical lines at x=n-2 and x=n-1, using two non-sunny lines. Then we have k=2 sunny lines left. The column n (size 1) can be covered by a sunny line (or a vertical line at x=n, but vertical line at x=n would be a third non-sunny line). But we have only n total lines, so we have n-2 non-sunny lines used (vertical at n-2 and n-1). The remaining n-2 lines are: 2 sunny lines and n-4 non-sunny lines. Actually total lines = n. We used 2 vertical lines (non-sunny). So we have n-2 lines left. Among those, we need exactly k=2 sunny lines, so the remaining n-4 lines are non-sunny. So we have n-4 non-sunny lines left to cover columns 1..n-3 (n-3 columns). So we have n-4 non-sunny lines for n-3 columns, which is one fewer than needed if we used only vertical lines. However, we have 2 sunny lines that can each cover one point in each column, reducing the need for vertical lines by at most 2 columns. So we can cover n-3 columns with n-4 vertical lines plus 2 sunny lines covering one point each column. But we need to ensure that the sunny lines can be placed such that they cover one point in each column (i.e., they intersect each column at some point in T_n). Since each sunny line can intersect each column at at most one point, we can assign each sunny line to cover a point in each column. So we could have the two sunny lines each covering one point per column, covering 2 points per column across the two lines. But each column has at most n+1-c points. For columns 1..n-3, the column sizes are at least 4 (since for c ≤ n-3, h_c = n+1-c ≥ 4). So each column has at least 4 points. So we can allocate one point per column to each sunny line, leaving at least 2 points per column uncovered, which can be covered by vertical lines. So it seems plausible that k=2 might be possible for n≥5. However, the solution claims k=2 impossible for all n≥3. Let's test with n=5.\n\nWe need to see if we can construct a configuration with n=5 lines, exactly k=2 sunny lines, covering all points (a,b) with a+b ≤ 6.\n\nPoints T_5: columns:\n\n- Column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) (5 points)\n- Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4) (4 points)\n- Column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) (3 points)\n- Column 4: (4,1),(4,2) (2 points)\n- Column 5: (5,1) (1 point)\n\nTotal points = 5+4+3+2+1 = 15 = n(n+1)/2 = 5*6/2 = 15.\n\nWe need 5 lines total, 2 sunny, 3 non-sunny. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Horizontal lines can cover at most one point per column (since they have constant y). Slope -1 lines also intersect each column at most once. So vertical lines are the only ones that can cover multiple points in a column. So we need to use vertical lines to cover columns with many points beyond the capacity of sunny lines.\n\nWe have 2 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So across the 2 sunny lines, we can cover at most 2 points per column. So for each column c, we need to cover h_c points. The remaining h_c - 2 points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Since we have 3 non-sunny lines, we need to allocate them to cover the remaining points.\n\nLet's compute h_c - 2 for each column:\n\n- Column 1: h_1 = 5, need to cover 5 - 2 = 3 points with non-sunny lines.\n- Column 2: h_2 = 4, need 2 points with non-sunny lines.\n- Column 3: h_3 = 3, need 1 point with non-sunny lines.\n- Column 4: h_4 = 2, need 0 points (since 2 - 2 = 0). So sunny lines can cover both points in column 4.\n- Column 5: h_5 = 1, need 0 points (since 1 - 2 = -1, but we just need to cover it with sunny lines, at most 1 point needed).\n\nThus we need to cover 3+2+1 = 6 points with non-sunny lines. We have 3 non-sunny lines. Each non-sunny line can cover at most one point per column (except vertical lines can cover many points in a column). So we need to allocate vertical lines to cover many points in a column. Since we need to cover 3 points in column 1, we could use a vertical line at x=1 to cover all 5 points in column 1, but that would also cover points that could be covered by sunny lines, but that's okay. However, we need to ensure we have distinct lines: we cannot have a vertical line at x=1 and also a sunny line that also passes through a point in column 1 (since each column 1 point must be covered by at least one line; overlapping coverage is allowed). So we could use a vertical line at x=1 to cover all points in column 1. Then we would not need sunny lines to cover any points in column 1. But we still need to cover points in other columns.\n\nIf we use vertical line at x=1, we have used 1 non-sunny line. We have 2 non-sunny lines left. We need to cover the remaining points: column 2 needs 2 points, column 3 needs 1 point. We could use vertical line at x=2 to cover all points in column 2 (4 points). That would cover the needed 2 points. Then we have used 2 non-sunny lines (vertical at x=1 and x=2). We have 1 non-sunny line left, which could be vertical at x=3 to cover column 3 (3 points). Then we have used all 3 non-sunny lines, covering columns 1,2,3 entirely. Then the 2 sunny lines can be used to cover columns 4 and 5 (and maybe also some points in columns 1-3, but they are already covered). So we could have a configuration: vertical lines x=1, x=2, x=3 (non-sunny), plus two sunny lines covering the points in columns 4 and 5. But we need to ensure that the sunny lines are distinct and not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or slope -1. Let's try to find two sunny lines that cover the points (4,1),(4,2),(5,1). That's 3 points. Since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, we need at least 2 lines to cover column 4's two points. So we need each sunny line to cover one point in column 4, and maybe also cover (5,1). So we need two lines: one through (4,1) and (5,1) would be horizontal (slope 0), not allowed. So we cannot have a line covering both (4,1) and (5,1). So we need separate lines for (4,1) and (4,2). Let's try:\n\n- Sunny line L1: through (4,1) and some other point not in column 4 (maybe (1, something) or (2, something) etc.) but we already have vertical lines covering columns 1-3, so we could use any other point. But we need to ensure L1 is sunny. For example, line through (4,1) and (1,2) has slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, which is sunny. So L1 can cover (4,1) and (1,2). But (1,2) is already covered by vertical line x=1, but that's okay.\n\n- Sunny line L2: through (4,2) and (1,3) maybe? Slope (2-3)/(4-1) = -1/3, same slope as L1? Actually (1,3) to (4,2) slope (2-3)/(4-1) = -1/3, same slope, but that's okay as long as lines are distinct. However, L2 would be parallel to L1 (both slope -1/3). Are parallel lines allowed? Yes, they are distinct lines, but they are parallel. That's fine. However, we need to ensure they are not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or line x+y=0. Slope -1/3 is not 0, ∞, -1, so they are sunny. So we can have L1: through (4,1) and (1,2); L2: through (4,2) and (1,3). Both are sunny. They also cover (5,1)? No, they don't cover (5,1). We still need to cover (5,1). We have used all 5 lines: vertical x=1, x=2, x=3, L1, L2. No line left to cover (5,1). So we need to adjust.\n\nWe could try to have one of the sunny lines also cover (5,1). For example, line through (5,1) and (1,2) has slope (1-2)/(5-1) = -1/4, sunny. That line would also cover (4,? )? At x=4, y = -1/4*4 + something? Let's compute line through (1,2) and (5,1): slope -1/4, equation y - 2 = -1/4 (x - 1) => y = -1/4 x + 2.25. At x=4, y = -1 + 2.25 = 1.25 (not integer). So it doesn't cover (4,1) or (4,2). So we could have L1: through (5,1) and (1,2) covering (5,1) and (1,2). L2: through (4,1) and (1,3) covering (4,1) and (1,3). Then we still need to cover (4,2). Could we have a vertical line at x=4? That's a non-sunny line, but we have used all non-sunny lines (vertical x=1,2,3). So we cannot add a vertical line at x=4. Could we have a sunny line covering (4,2) and some other point? Yes, we could have L3, but we only have 2 sunny lines. So we need to cover (4,2) with one of the existing sunny lines. Could we adjust L1 or L2 to also pass through (4,2)? Let's see if we can find a sunny line that passes through (5,1) and (4,2). Slope = (2-1)/(4-5) = 1/(-1) = -1, which is not allowed (slope -1). So cannot.\n\nCould we have a sunny line that passes through (4,2) and (1,2)? That would be horizontal (slope 0), not allowed. (4,2) and (1,3) slope = (2-3)/(4-1) = -1/3, allowed. So L2 could be (4,2)-(1,3). Then we need to cover (4,1) and (5,1). Could we have a sunny line that passes through (4,1) and (5,1)? That's horizontal, not allowed. So we need a line that passes through (4,1) and some other point, maybe (1,4) (if n≥4). For n=5, (1,4) is in T_5. Slope (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So cannot. (4,1) and (1,5) slope = (1-5)/(4-1) = -4/3, allowed. So line through (4,1) and (1,5) is sunny. That line also passes through (5,? )? At x=5, y = -4/3* (5-1) + 1? Let's compute: line through (1,5) and (4,1): slope = (1-5)/(4-1) = -4/3. Equation: y - 5 = -4/3 (x - 1) => y = -4/3 x + 4/3 + 5 = -4/3 x + 19/3. At x=5, y = -20/3 + 19/3 = -1/3, not integer. So line does not cover (5,1). So we need a separate line for (5,1). But we have only 2 sunny lines.\n\nThus this attempt fails.\n\nMaybe we can use vertical lines at x=1, x=2, and a non-vertical non-sunny line (horizontal or slope -1) to cover column 3 or 4. But we have only 3 non-sunny lines total. Let's try a different allocation: Use vertical lines at x=1 and x=2 (cover columns 1 and 2). Use a slope -1 line to cover some points in column 3 and maybe column 4,5. Then use 2 sunny lines to cover the rest. Let's explore.\n\nWe need to cover column 3 (points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)). A slope -1 line can intersect column 3 at at most one point. So it cannot cover all three points. So we need vertical line at x=3 or sunny lines to cover them. Since we have only 2 sunny lines, they can cover at most 2 points in column 3. So we need a vertical line at x=3 to cover the remaining point. So we need vertical lines at x=1,2,3 (3 vertical lines). That's already 3 non-sunny lines, leaving 2 sunny lines. So we are back to the earlier scenario: vertical lines at x=1,2,3, sunny lines covering columns 4 and 5. But we saw that we cannot cover (4,1),(4,2),(5,1) with only 2 sunny lines. Let's see if we can cover them with 2 sunny lines: we need to cover 3 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we need at least 2 lines to cover column 4's two points, and we also need to cover column 5's point. So we need at least 3 lines (since column 4 has 2 points, each line can cover at most one, so we need at least 2 lines for column 4, and we need a third line for column 5 unless one of the lines covering column 4 also covers column 5. But a line can intersect column 5 as well. So we could have a line that covers (4,1) and (5,1) but that's horizontal, not allowed. Or a line that covers (4,2) and (5,1) maybe? Slope = (1-2)/(5-4) = -1, not allowed. So any line that covers a point in column 5 (5,1) and a point in column 4 will have slope (1 - y4)/(5 - 4) = 1 - y4. For y4 = 1, slope = 0 (horizontal), not allowed. For y4 = 2, slope = -1, not allowed. So any line that passes through (5,1) and a point in column 4 will be either horizontal or slope -1, both not sunny. So (5,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with any point in column 4. So (5,1) must be covered by a sunny line that does not intersect column 4 (i.e., a line that passes through (5,1) and some point in column ≤3). But columns 1-3 are covered by vertical lines, but we can still use them as points for the sunny line. So we could have a sunny line through (5,1) and (1,2) (slope -1/4). That line would not intersect column 4 (since at x=4, y = -1/4*4 + something = maybe not integer). So (5,1) can be covered by a sunny line that does not intersect column 4. Then we need to cover (4,1) and (4,2) with the remaining sunny line. But a single line cannot cover both (4,1) and (4,2) because they have same x-coordinate (vertical) which is not sunny. So we need two separate lines for (4,1) and (4,2). But we have only one sunny line left (since we used one for (5,1)). So impossible. Therefore k=2 is impossible for n=5.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k=2 is impossible for all n≥3 seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to verify the solution's argument for k≥4. The solution's argument is less rigorous but we can see that for n≥4, k≥4 is impossible because we can reduce to T_4 subproblem. As we argued, T_4 cannot be covered by 4 sunny lines. So for any n≥4, if we have k≥4 sunny lines, we can consider the rightmost 4 columns (columns n-3,...,n). The points in those columns form a set isomorphic to T_4 (by translation). Since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, the restriction of the lines to those columns yields a covering of T_4 by k' ≤ k sunny lines (since we may have some vertical lines that intersect those columns, but vertical lines are non-sunny, so they cannot be used to cover points in those columns if we only have sunny lines? Actually vertical lines are non-sunny, but we could have vertical lines among the n lines. However, the condition is that exactly k of the lines are sunny. So if k≥4, we have at least 4 sunny lines, but we could also have vertical lines among the n lines. The vertical lines could be used to cover some points in the rightmost 4 columns. However, the rightmost 4 columns have sizes: column n-3 has 4 points, column n-2 has 3 points, column n-1 has 2 points, column n has 1 point. So total 10 points. If we have vertical lines at some of those columns, they would be non-sunny lines, but we have only n - k non-sunny lines. For k≥4, n - k ≤ n - 4. For n large, we could have many non-sunny lines. But the argument in the solution seems to assume that we cannot use vertical lines for those columns because we need to use vertical lines for the three rightmost columns already? Actually they argue that the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2 (size 3). But if k≥4, we have at least 4 sunny lines, so we could use vertical lines for column n-2 and maybe also for column n-1 or n-3. But we need to check if that could allow k≥4.\n\nLet's examine the possibility of k=4 for n=5. We have 5 lines total, 4 sunny, 1 non-sunny. The non-sunny line could be vertical at column n-2 (i.e., x=3). Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover the remaining points. The rightmost 4 columns are columns 2,3,4,5? Actually for n=5, rightmost 4 columns are columns 2,3,4,5 (since n-3=2). The points in those columns are:\n\n- Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4) (4 points)\n- Column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) (3 points)\n- Column 4: (4,1),(4,2) (2 points)\n- Column 5: (5,1) (1 point)\n\nTotal 10 points. We have 4 sunny lines and 1 vertical line at x=3 (covering column 3). The vertical line covers (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). So we need to cover the remaining 7 points with 4 sunny lines. The columns left are 2,4,5 (and column 3 partially covered). Column 2 has 4 points, column 4 has 2 points, column 5 has 1 point. So we need to cover 4+2+1 = 7 points with 4 sunny lines. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most once. So across 4 lines, we can cover at most 4 points in column 2, which is enough. In column 4, we can cover at most 4 points, but we only need 2. In column 5, at most 4 points, need 1. So capacity is fine. However, we need to ensure collinearity constraints.\n\nWe need to find 4 sunny lines that cover all points in columns 2,4,5 (and maybe some points in column 3 not covered by vertical line). Since we have vertical line covering all points in column 3, we don't need to cover them with sunny lines. So we need to cover points:\n\n- Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4)\n- Column 4: (4,1),(4,2)\n- Column 5: (5,1)\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines. Let's try to assign each column's points to lines.\n\nWe need to cover column 2's 4 points, so each of the 4 sunny lines must cover exactly one point in column 2. So each sunny line will be assigned a distinct point from column 2. Then we need to cover column 4's 2 points and column 5's 1 point. Since each line can also intersect column 4 and column 5, we can assign some of the sunny lines to also cover points in those columns.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines, each passing through a distinct point in column 2, and also possibly covering points in columns 4 and 5. Let's denote the column 2 points as B1=(2,1), B2=(2,2), B3=(2,3), B4=(2,4). We need to assign each to a line L_i.\n\nNow we need to see if we can choose lines L_i such that they also pass through points in column 4 or 5.\n\nWe need to cover (4,1),(4,2),(5,1). Let's see which column 2 points can be paired with which column 4 or 5 points via a sunny line.\n\nCompute slopes:\n\n- Pair B1 (2,1) with (4,1): slope (1-1)/(4-2) = 0, horizontal, not allowed.\n- B1 with (4,2): slope (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 = 0.5, allowed. So line through (2,1) and (4,2) is sunny.\n\n- B1 with (5,1): slope (1-1)/(5-2) = 0, horizontal, not allowed.\n\n- B2 (2,2) with (4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed.\n\n- B2 with (4,2): slope (2-2)/(4-2) = 0, horizontal, not allowed.\n\n- B2 with (5,1): slope (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, allowed.\n\n- B3 (2,3) with (4,1): slope (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\n- B3 with (4,2): slope (2-3)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed.\n\n- B3 with (5,1): slope (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3 ≈ -0.667, allowed.\n\n- B4 (2,4) with (4,1): slope (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed.\n\n- B4 with (4,2): slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\n- B4 with (5,1): slope (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus possible pairings:\n\n- B1 can pair with (4,2) (slope 0.5)\n- B2 can pair with (4,1) (slope -0.5) or (5,1) (slope -1/3)\n- B3 can pair with (4,2) (slope -0.5) or (5,1) (slope -2/3)\n- B4 can pair with (4,1) (slope -1.5)\n\nThus we can try to assign lines:\n\n- L1: B1 (2,1) with (4,2) (covers B1 and (4,2))\n- L2: B2 (2,2) with (4,1) (covers B2 and (4,1))\n- L3: B3 (2,3) with (5,1) (covers B3 and (5,1))\n- L4: B4 (2,4) alone (covers B4 only)\n\nNow we have covered all points: B1,B2,B3,B4, (4,1),(4,2),(5,1). All are covered. Are the lines sunny? Let's check slopes:\n\n- L1: slope (2-1)/(4-2) = 0.5, sunny.\n- L2: slope (1-2)/(4-2) = -0.5, sunny.\n- L3: slope (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, sunny.\n- L4: we need to define a line through B4 (2,4) and some other point not in T_5 (maybe (1, something) or (3, something)). We can choose any point that yields a sunny slope. For example, line through (2,4) and (1,1) has slope (4-1)/(2-1) = 3, sunny. So L4: line through (2,4) and (1,1) slope 3, sunny. This line also passes through (3,7) (outside). So L4 is sunny.\n\nThus we have a configuration with n=5 lines: vertical line x=3 (non-sunny), sunny lines L1, L2, L3, L4 as defined. That's 4 sunny lines, 1 non-sunny line, total 5 lines. Does this cover all points? Let's verify:\n\n- Column 1: points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). Are they covered? We have vertical line x=3 covering column 3 only. So column 1 points are not covered yet. Wait we haven't covered column 1 points. The lines L1-L4 may intersect column 1 at some points. Let's check:\n\n- L1: line through (2,1) and (4,2). Equation: slope 0.5, passes through (2,1). At x=1, y = 1 - 0.5 = 0.5 (not integer). So L1 does not cover any column 1 point.\n\n- L2: line through (2,2) and (4,1). Slope -0.5, passes through (2,2). At x=1, y = 2 + 0.5 = 2.5 (not integer). So L2 does not cover column 1.\n\n- L3: line through (2,3) and (5,1). Slope -2/3, passes through (2,3). At x=1, y = 3 + 2/3 = 3.666..., not integer.\n\n- L4: line through (2,4) and (1,1). Slope 3, passes through (2,4). At x=1, y = 1 (since we defined it through (1,1)). So L4 covers (1,1). Good.\n\nThus column 1 points (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) are not covered. We need to cover them. We have used all 5 lines (vertical x=3, L1-L4). So we cannot add more lines. So this configuration fails to cover column 1 points.\n\nThus we need to adjust the allocation: we need to cover column 1 points as well. Since we have only 5 lines total, we need to allocate some lines to cover column 1 points. But we already used vertical line x=3 (non-sunny) to cover column 3. We could use a vertical line at x=1 (non-sunny) to cover column 1, but that would be a second non-sunny line, leaving only 3 sunny lines (since k=4). But we need exactly k=4 sunny lines, so we can have only 1 non-sunny line. So we cannot have a vertical line at x=1. So we need to cover column 1 points using sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point in column 1. Since we have 4 sunny lines, we can cover at most 4 points in column 1. Column 1 has 5 points. So we cannot cover all column 1 points with only 4 sunny lines. Therefore k=4 is impossible for n=5. This argument generalizes: For any n≥4, column 1 has n points. With k sunny lines, we can cover at most k points in column 1. The remaining n - k points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Since we have n - k non-sunny lines total, we could use vertical lines for those columns. However, we need to allocate vertical lines to columns where we need to cover many points. For column 1, we could use a vertical line at x=1 (non-sunny) to cover all n points in column 1. That would use one non-sunny line. Then we would have n - k - 1 non-sunny lines left for other columns. But we also need to cover other columns. Let's see if we can allocate vertical lines to cover columns 1,...,some. The key is that we have n - k non-sunny lines total. If k≥4, then n - k ≤ n - 4. So we have at most n - 4 non-sunny lines. To cover column 1, we could use one vertical line. Then we have n - k - 1 ≤ n - 5 non-sunny lines left for the remaining columns. The remaining columns are columns 2,...,n. The total number of columns is n - 1. We have n - k - 1 non-sunny lines left, which is at most n - 5. So we have at most n - 5 vertical lines left to cover n - 1 columns (excluding column 1). So we would need to rely on sunny lines to cover points in some of those columns. But each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. Since we have k sunny lines, we can cover at most k points per column. For columns with many points, we may need vertical lines. But we need to check if this is possible.\n\nBut the solution's argument for k≥4 is not fully rigorous, but we can see that for n≥4, k≥4 is impossible because column 1 has n points, and we have only k sunny lines, so we need at least n - k non-sunny lines to cover the remaining points in column 1. Since we have exactly n - k non-sunny lines total, we could allocate all of them to column 1 (vertical lines at x=1). But we cannot have multiple vertical lines at the same column (they would be the same line). So we can only have one vertical line at x=1. So we cannot cover all n points in column 1 with vertical lines unless we have a vertical line at x=1 (covers all points in column 1). That's one non-sunny line. Then we have n - k - 1 non-sunny lines left for other columns. But we need to cover the remaining points in column 1 (if any) with sunny lines. Since we have k sunny lines, we can cover at most k points in column 1. So we need n - k ≤ k? Actually we need to cover n points in column 1. Using one vertical line covers all n points, so we don't need sunny lines for column 1. So we can allocate the vertical line to column 1, and then we have k sunny lines and n - k - 1 non-sunny lines left. But we need to cover columns 2..n. Let's see if we can do that.\n\nFor n=5, k=4, we have n - k = 1 non-sunny line. So we can only have one vertical line. If we use it at column 1, we cover column 1 entirely. Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover columns 2..5. Column 2 has 4 points, column 3 has 3 points, column 4 has 2 points, column 5 has 1 point. The 4 sunny lines can each cover at most one point per column. So across 4 lines, we can cover at most 4 points in column 2, which is enough. In column 3, we can cover at most 4 points, need 3, okay. Column 